{
  "assessment": "The source provides a quantitative relation for the average inverse shock time for random phases in the FPUT chain, with a clear control (number of excited modes p) and readout (average inverse shock time). The main result is an asymptotic scaling law with a logarithmic correction, supported by numerical simulations. The extraction focuses on this single quantitative cluster, including the coherent-phase comparison as a secondary regime. The case is authoring-ready with one numeric control and one numeric readout.",
  "case_authoring_ready": true,
  "source": {
    "citation": "Matteo Gallone, Random Initial Data and Average Shock Time in the Fermi-Pasta-Ulam-Tsingou Chain, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/qxzs-1t1l",
    "journal_doi": "10.1103/qxzs-1t1l",
    "arxiv_versioned_id": "2511.07664v2"
  },
  "paper_scope": "The paper studies the Fermi-Pasta-Ulam-Tsingou (FPUT) chain with long-wavelength random initial data, focusing on the formation of a prethermal state characterized by a Burgers-type shock and a turbulent-like spectrum. The main quantitative result is an asymptotic formula for the average shock time in the thermodynamic limit, scaling as (p sqrt(log p))^{-1} for large p, where p is the number of initially excited modes. The paper also compares with coherent-phase initial data and discusses the connection to the Galerkin-truncated Burgers equation.",
  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "mechanism_id": "burgers_approximation",
      "statement": "The FPUT dynamics is approximated by a pair of inviscid Burgers equations for the renormalized fields lambda and rho, valid for small specific energy and large N. The shock time is determined by the maximum of the initial derivative of these fields.",
      "equations": [
        "lambda_tau = (1 + alpha sqrt(epsilon)/sqrt(2) lambda) lambda_x",
        "rho_tau = -(1 + alpha sqrt(epsilon)/sqrt(2) rho) rho_x",
        "(tau_s^eta)^{-1} = alpha sqrt(epsilon)/sqrt(2) max_x eta_x(x), eta = lambda, rho"
      ],
      "variable_names": [
        "lambda",
        "rho",
        "tau_s",
        "alpha",
        "epsilon",
        "eta_x"
      ],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "alpha_coeff",
          "value": 1.0,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "Coefficient in the Burgers equation and shock time formula; set to 1 in numerical simulations.",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 190,
              "line_end": 191,
              "support": "Burgers equations with coefficient alpha sqrt(epsilon)/sqrt(2)."
            },
            {
              "line_start": 199,
              "line_end": 200,
              "support": "Shock time formula with alpha sqrt(epsilon)/sqrt(2)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "sqrt2_factor",
          "value": 1.4142135623730951,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "Factor 1/sqrt(2) in the shock time formula.",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 199,
              "line_end": 200,
              "support": "Formula includes division by sqrt(2)."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 190,
          "line_end": 191,
          "support": "Burgers equations for lambda and rho."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 199,
          "line_end": 200,
          "support": "Shock time formula."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "random_phase_statistics",
      "statement": "For random phases, the average inverse shock time is proportional to the expectation of the maximum of a random trigonometric polynomial. The extremal statistics are analyzed using chaining and metric entropy bounds, yielding a sharp asymptotic scaling.",
      "equations": [
        "E[ (tau_s^omega)^{-1} ] ~ alpha sqrt(epsilon) p sqrt(log p) / N",
        "E[ max_x f_p^omega(x) ] ~ p^{3/2} sqrt(log p)",
        "d_p(x,y)^2 = 1/2 sum_{k=1}^p k^2 (1 - cos(2 pi k (x-y)))"
      ],
      "variable_names": [
        "f_p^omega",
        "d_p",
        "X_p^omega",
        "tau_s^omega"
      ],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "log_power",
